rding to the report.\n- e432 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia aims to begin RTX Pro 5500 shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, a plan reported by the article via The Information rather than in an Nvidia disclosure; at that rate a single ByteDance order of roughly 1 million units would absorb about two quarters of planned China output.\n    Quote: Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\n- e433 [fact; finance.biggo.com (citing TechPowerUp for specifications); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia released the RTX Pro 5500 this month (September 2026), a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp.\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU that Nvidia released this month. It carries 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e434 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Industry executives (unidentified) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Industry executives believe the RTX Pro 5500, because it sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, may not be subject to the same restrictions that barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from China; the article notes Washington has not said whether the card may be shipped to China at all and that the exclusion is the executives' reading, not a published position