anghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n    Quote: Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e441 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance (Volcano Engine) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance's cloud unit Volcano Engine said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n    Quote: ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n- e442 [party_statement; CNBC (Greer interview), as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Jamieson Greer, U.S. Trade Representative; China Ministry of Foreign Affairs ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts of last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington; USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \"taken off the table\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight summit deliverables.\n    Quote: The signal from Beijing follows last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington, where chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts. USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \"taken off the table\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e519 [rumour; insidermonkey.com; original source The Information (as reported by Insider Monkey) ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] The Information reported that Chinese officials have asked ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans for buying Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 processors, a signal that Beijing is weighing whether to permit the purchases. The article states that neither government has confirmed anything and that the report describes deliberations rather than a decision.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) may be allowed to sell part of its chip range in China again. Officials there have asked ByteDance and Alibaba Group Holding Limited (NYSE: BABA ) to report their plans for buying its RTX PRO 5500 processors, according to The Information. … Governments ask for purchase plans for one reason. They are deciding whether to permit the purchases. Neither government has\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e520 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source Alibaba Group Holding Limited ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] Alibaba unveiled its own AI accelerator this month, which it presents as running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced; the article adds that several Chinese firms are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago as they design around Nvidia.\n    Quote: That policy has produced results. Alibaba unveiled its own accelerator this month, running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced. Chinese firms have been designing around Nvidia rather than waiting for export rules to loosen, and several are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e53; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e566 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:726f5fb49c3ec6e1d67d3a65 2026-09-28] Chinese regulators mostly blocked imports of Nvidia's H200 processors even after the Trump administration approved the exports, the article reports, illustrating that Nvidia's China sales depend on decisions by both governments. No date is given.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports even after the Trump administration approved exports.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e81; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is rung 3: the supplied reporting describes consideration and intent, not a formal approval or rejection, and no market consensus is supplied. For the forecast, the specific report that officials told some firms purchases were likely to receive approval and that MIIT intended to approve them makes at least one formal approval by January 31, 2027 the most likely outcome. The roughly 1 million-unit ByteDance plan and reported 500,000-unit quarterly China supply target support the commercial case, but are not approvals or confirmed orders; repeated coverage of the same underlying report is not counted as independent confirmation. U.S. export uncertainty and China's history of restricting imports temper confidence, but do not establish a formal rejection or abandonment. Since run\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\nQUESTION Q3 {'ref': 'Q3'}\nThesis v3 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By January 31, 2027, U.S. authorities authorize exports of Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow Nvidia’s shipments into China. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 23, 2026, the U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security formally prohibits Blackwell exports to China through January 31, 2027, or Chinese authorities formally prohibit Nvidia’s Blackwell shipments into China through that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 84 items]\n[Not shown: 29 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\n    Quote: The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e365, e425, e565, e745, e851, e879, e942; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\n    Quote: Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e566; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e82 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Nvidia has not factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, according to the article — meaning any resumption of China shipments would be incremental to its stated outlook.\n    Quote: While Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\n- e86 [fact; barchart.com; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) 2026-09-23; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is scheduled to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, 2026, along with other technology leaders, as the U.S. and China discuss AI, trade, tariffs and advanced semiconductors. The article says the dinner itself does not guarantee policy changes but could provide a backdrop for signals on NVIDIA's access to the Chinese market and export restrictions.\n    Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e302; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e111 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, a guidance the article says excludes China Data Center compute revenue, cited as geopolitical uncertainty.\n    Quote: Management guided Q3 to $108 billion. … Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\n  